# jobhunt workspace — notes for Claude

This is a personal jobhunt *workspace*: profile, CV, ratings, digests. The code is the `jobhunt`
package that `pyproject.toml` installs from GitHub; there is no source here to edit. Everything
in this directory is personal data — never publish it.

## Layout

| path | who writes it |
|------|---------------|
| `profile/profile.md` | the user — who they are, what they want, what rules a role out |
| `profile/preferences.md` | `## Manual` the user, never touched by the engine; `## Learned` (general patterns) and `## Specifics` (one-off inferences) are regenerated from ratings by `jobhunt rate` — don't hand-edit those |
| `docs/cv.md` | the user (or `scripts/extract-cv.sh` from a PDF in `docs/`); the scorer reads it |
| `config/sources.yaml` | the user — where to look: queries per source, `pages:` entries, optional `contact:` |
| `config/settings.yaml` | the user — how results are shown and how the pipeline behaves (theme, score range, digest size, scoring model, what the Rated page hides, update checks); the UI's Settings page rewrites it |
| `digests/YYYY-MM-DD[-N].md` | generated by `check`/`digest`; the user fills in `rating:` and `note:` lines here |
| `shortlist.md` | regenerated: open listings rated 4–5 |
| `data/ratings.jsonl` | append-only rating log, the durable record — never edit or rewrite it |
| `data/jobs.sqlite` | every listing seen plus scores; a rebuildable cache, gitignored |

The newest digest is the highest `YYYY-MM-DD`, then the highest `-N` (`-2` sorts *before*
`.md` alphabetically, so don't trust `ls | tail`).

## Rules

- `/jobhunt` runs the pipeline, presents digests, discusses roles and records ratings given in
  chat — use it rather than scoring or ranking listings yourself; `jobhunt score` does that so
  results stay reproducible and the ratings loop stays intact.
- Talking about a role (is this worth applying to?) is part of the job: read
  `profile/profile.md`, `profile/preferences.md` and `docs/cv.md` first, and start from what
  the engine already decided — `uv run jobhunt show <url-or-id>` prints a listing's score, why
  and concerns. Disagree with it out loud rather than quietly replacing it.
- A link the user pastes: `uv run jobhunt add <url>` fetches, stores and scores it, so it joins
  the queue and the ratings loop instead of living in the chat. A whole new site to watch is
  `/jobhunt-sources` (a `pages:` entry in `config/sources.yaml`), never engine code in `.venv`.
- The user disagreeing with an inferred rule is a `## Manual` rule, not an edit to `## Learned`
  or `## Specifics` — those are regenerated and your edit would vanish. Draft it, get their
  yes, write it under `## Manual`, then `uv run jobhunt learn`.
- `uv run jobhunt serve` is a browser UI for the same pipeline; it runs until Ctrl-C, so start
  it in a terminal the user controls, not from a tool call that waits for exit. Ratings made
  there land in `data/ratings.jsonl` like `jobhunt rate` does.
- Editing `profile/profile.md` or `profile/preferences.md` only affects listings scored from
  then on; run `uv run jobhunt check --rescore` to re-score everything still open (one Claude
  call per 10 listings — it takes a while).
- Scoring and preference learning go through the shell `claude` CLI (`claude -p`); if output
  mentions `OAuth session expired`, the user has to run `claude login` in a terminal. Don't call
  `claude -p` yourself to diagnose.
- Never work around scraper blocks (403/CAPTCHA/sign-in walls) — surface the manual link
  instead.
- A newer engine: the UI shows a banner with an *Update & restart* button, or run
  `uv run jobhunt update`. If no banner ever appears, `uv run jobhunt update --check` says why
  (a development checkout, a commit pin in `pyproject.toml`, an install that did not come from
  git, or the git error itself); `uv sync --upgrade-package jobhunt` is the fallback.
- Engine bugs and new sources belong in the engine repo, not here: don't patch `.venv`. The
  dashboard's *Report a bug* / *Suggest a feature* links open a prefilled issue, or go to
  https://github.com/jessefontaine/jobhunt/issues.
